C2 phase 3 — the router: stateless write sharding
2026-08-10 · router + ingester-a + ingester-b (bench/compose/timelakedb-cluster.yml)
================================================================================

THE ROUTER is the single public write endpoint (FR-8/FR-9) — clients point
at one address and never at specific nodes. It holds NO data. It shards each
line-protocol body by (db, measurement) across the ingesters and forwards
each shard to the ingester that owns that table; that ingester becomes the
table's primary and replicates to its CL-2 peer (phase 2), so durability is
unchanged. The router adds distribution, not a new failure mode.

Drilled: router in front of the ingester pair, 12 tables x 500 lines all
written through the single router endpoint.

  8/8 checks passed

  - Every table lives on exactly ONE primary — the other ingester holds a
    dormant replica (0 queryable), so no double-count.
  - Exact accounting: all 6,000 acked lines present once — no loss, no dup.
  - DISTRIBUTED: 6 tables landed primary on A, 6 on B. The hash actually
    spreads load; the router is not a pass-through to one node.
  - The router forwarded a mixed body to >1 shard in one request
    (forwarded_total counts sub-requests: one body touching both ingesters
    = 2 forwards).
  - timelake_router_ingesters = 2.

  ATOMICITY PRESERVED across sharding. A body with a poison line returned
  400 at the router and wrote NOTHING — the router validates the whole body
  before forwarding anything, so a bad line never leaves part of the batch
  written on one shard. (An infrastructure failure of one shard is returned
  to the client instead; the retry is safe because writes are idempotent
  under LWW dedup.)

  QUERIES ARE NOT ROUTED. /api/sql on the router returns 501. In a sharded
  cluster a query is only correct once a querier unions every shard from the
  shared object store; forwarding a query to one ingester would return wrong
  counts. Query routing arrives with the querier (CL-3, C2 phase 4).

HOW SHARDING WORKS
  - shard_of(db, measurement) = FNV-1a over `db\0measurement` mod N. Stable
    across processes/restarts (not the randomized default hasher), and the
    router sorts ingesters by id, so a table always lands on the same node.
  - The router hashes to ANY ingester — there is no fixed primary/replica.
    A table's primary is whichever ingester its hash selects; that node
    replicates to its own CL-2 peer. So the two-node pair already shows real
    distribution: some tables primary on A (replica on B), some the reverse.

role=all AND role=ingester ARE UNCHANGED — the router is a separate code
path (main.rs branches before the engine is even created; a router opens no
engine, no WAL, no store). Pinned by the full suite and a role=all smoke.

SCOPE / NOT YET
  - Query and Flight forwarding: phase 4 (needs the querier).
  - Automatic failover of a dead ingester's shard: later (needs health).
  - Intra-cluster/forward transport is plaintext HTTP at C2; mTLS at C3.

WORKSPACE: 141 tests (+5 router unit: measurement extraction incl. escaping,
deterministic + distributing hash, stable target ordering; +1 cluster peer
data-address), clippy clean, fmt clean.
